  <!-- SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
  <section class="section" id="recommendations">
    <h2><span class="section-num">6</span> Strategic Recommendations</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Actionable recommendations synthesized directly from the data. Each recommendation references specific findings from this analysis.</p>

    <div class="rec-grid">
      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-green">Sourcing</span> Buy Apple in the UK, Sell in US/AU</h4>
        <p>The UK offers Apple Watch listings at a $393 median — 28.6% below the global median of $550. Australia ($792) and the US ($550) represent strong sell-side markets. Even after shipping and fees, this spread creates meaningful margin.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> Apple UK median $392.74 vs AU $792.00 — $399 spread (Section 3)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-green">Sourcing</span> Source Samsung from Japan</h4>
        <p>Samsung's Japan median ($437) is 17.5% below the global median. The UK Samsung market trades at $825 — the largest single-brand geographic spread in the dataset at $388. Source in Japan, sell in the UK.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> Samsung Japan $437 vs UK $825 — 55.7% premium in UK (Section 3)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-blue">Pricing</span> Prioritize "New" Condition for Apple Only</h4>
        <p>Apple is the only brand where condition creates a meaningful price premium (17.7% for new vs used). For Garmin and Samsung, the new-vs-used premium is effectively zero — invest in condition grading and presentation only for Apple inventory.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> Apple new/used premium 17.7%, Garmin -0.3%, Samsung +0.6% (Section 2)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-blue">Inventory</span> Focus Garmin for Predictable Margins</h4>
        <p>Garmin's remarkably tight pricing distribution ($198 std dev) means margin forecasts are reliable. Power sellers already over-index on Garmin 4.8x — following their lead reduces pricing risk. The $215 minimum floor across the dataset means little downside exposure.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> Garmin std dev $198 vs Apple $834; power sellers 34.8% Garmin vs 7.2% for casual sellers (Sections 2, 5)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-amber">Product</span> Target the 47–52mm Segment</h4>
        <p>The 47–52mm bracket (Apple Watch Ultra, Garmin Fenix/Epix) has the highest median price ($594) and represents 39% of size-reported listings. This is the most liquid, highest-value form factor in the secondary market.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> 47-52mm median $594 (1,066 listings), 49mm alone is 36.4% of sized inventory (Section 4)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4><span class="badge badge-amber">Product</span> Explore Garmin Small-Case Niche</h4>
        <p>Garmin watches under 40mm carry a surprising $735 median — the highest of any brand-size combination. This niche (26 listings) is small but high-value. Limited supply and strong resale suggest an underserved segment worth monitoring.</p>
        <div class="evidence"><strong>Data ref:</strong> Garmin &lt;40mm median $735 from 26 listings (Section 4)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
